insert into public.flashcards (id, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
('f3-001','core',3,'3.1',$$Cloud responsibility matrix - who secures what?$$,$$Provider secures the cloud (hardware, facilities, hypervisor); customer secures what is IN the cloud (data, identities, configs). Split varies by IaaS/PaaS/SaaS.$$),
('f3-002','core',3,'3.1',$$In SaaS vs IaaS, who patches the OS?$$,$$IaaS: the customer patches guest OSes. SaaS: the provider handles everything below (and including) the application.$$),
('f3-003','core',3,'3.1',$$What is a hybrid cloud and its main security consideration?$$,$$Mix of on-prem/private and public cloud; consistent identity, policy, and visibility across both environments is the challenge.$$),
('f3-004','core',3,'3.1',$$Security pros/cons of serverless?$$,$$Pros: no OS to patch, tiny attack surface per function, provider hardening. Cons: dependency on provider, IAM misconfig, event-injection risks, limited visibility.$$),
('f3-005','core',3,'3.1',$$What are microservices and their security trade-off?$$,$$App decomposed into small networked services; isolation and independent updates, but a much larger east-west attack surface - service-to-service authN (mTLS) needed.$$),
('f3-006','core',3,'3.1',$$Infrastructure as Code - security benefits and risks?$$,$$Benefits: versioned, reviewable, repeatable secure configs; drift detection. Risks: secrets in templates, a bad template propagates a flaw everywhere instantly.$$),
('f3-007','core',3,'3.1',$$Container security - what is unique vs VMs?$$,$$Containers share the host kernel (weaker isolation than VMs); image supply-chain (registry) trust, container escape, and host hardening are key concerns.$$),
('f3-008','core',3,'3.1',$$What is network infrastructure "physical isolation" vs "logical segmentation"?$$,$$Physical: separate hardware/air gap. Logical: VLANs/subnets/SDN separating traffic on shared hardware.$$),
('f3-009','core',3,'3.1',$$ICS/SCADA security priorities - how do they differ from IT?$$,$$Availability and safety outrank confidentiality; systems are legacy, fragile to scanning, rarely patchable - isolate, monitor passively, control access.$$),
('f3-010','core',3,'3.1',$$IoT/embedded device security challenges?$$,$$Default credentials, unpatchable firmware, long lifespans, weak crypto - mitigate with network segmentation and inventory.$$),
('f3-011','core',3,'3.1',$$High availability vs fault tolerance?$$,$$HA: minimal downtime via redundancy/failover (may blip). Fault tolerance: zero interruption - component failure is invisible (e.g., RAID 1, dual PSU).$$),
('f3-012','core',3,'3.1',$$Considerations: resilience vs cost vs complexity (exam framing)?$$,$$More availability = more cost/complexity; architecture decisions weigh availability, resilience, cost, responsiveness, scalability, ease of deployment/recovery, patchability, power/compute.$$),
('f3-013','core',3,'3.2',$$What is a screened subnet (DMZ)?$$,$$A buffer network between internet and LAN hosting public-facing services; firewalls on both sides so DMZ compromise does not reach the internal network.$$),
('f3-014','core',3,'3.2',$$What is attack surface in infrastructure terms?$$,$$Every point where an attacker can interact: open ports, services, interfaces, connections - minimize by disabling/closing what is not needed.$$),
('f3-015','core',3,'3.2',$$Fail-open vs fail-closed - when do you want each?$$,$$Fail-closed (block on failure) for security-critical controls like firewalls; fail-open (allow on failure) where availability/safety outweighs, e.g., some IPS or door egress.$$),
('f3-016','core',3,'3.2',$$Inline (active) vs tap/monitor (passive) devices?$$,$$Inline: traffic flows THROUGH the device (IPS - can block, adds latency/failure point). Passive: device sees a COPY via tap/SPAN (IDS - detect only).$$),
('f3-017','core',3,'3.2',$$Layer 4 vs Layer 7 firewall?$$,$$L4: filters by IP/port/protocol (fast, no app awareness). L7/NGFW: inspects application content, users, apps - deeper control, more processing.$$),
('f3-018','core',3,'3.2',$$What is a jump server?$$,$$A hardened, monitored intermediary host admins must pass through to reach sensitive/segmented systems - single controlled entry point.$$),
('f3-019','core',3,'3.2',$$Forward proxy vs reverse proxy?$$,$$Forward: sits in front of CLIENTS going out (filtering, anonymity). Reverse: sits in front of SERVERS receiving traffic (load balancing, TLS termination, WAF).$$),
('f3-020','core',3,'3.2',$$Active/active vs active/passive load balancing?$$,$$Active/active: all nodes serve traffic (max capacity). Active/passive: standby node takes over on failure (simpler, capacity idle).$$),
('f3-021','core',3,'3.2',$$What is 802.1X?$$,$$Port-based network access control: supplicant (device) authenticates via authenticator (switch/AP) to an authentication server (RADIUS) before getting network access.$$),
('f3-022','core',3,'3.2',$$IPSec: AH vs ESP, tunnel vs transport?$$,$$AH: integrity/authentication only, no encryption. ESP: encryption + integrity. Tunnel mode: whole packet encapsulated (site-to-site VPN). Transport: payload only (host-to-host).$$),
('f3-023','core',3,'3.2',$$Full tunnel vs split tunnel VPN?$$,$$Full: ALL client traffic goes through the VPN (inspectable, slower). Split: only corporate traffic tunnels, internet goes direct (faster, less visibility/control).$$),
('f3-024','core',3,'3.2',$$What is SD-WAN and why do orgs adopt it?$$,$$Policy-driven WAN over mixed links (broadband, LTE, MPLS) with central control - cheaper and more flexible than MPLS-only; pair with security (often SASE).$$),
('f3-025','core',3,'3.2',$$What is SASE?$$,$$Secure Access Service Edge: cloud-delivered bundle of networking + security (SD-WAN, ZTNA, SWG, CASB, FWaaS) applied consistently wherever users are.$$),
('f3-026','core',3,'3.2',$$What is TLS inspection and its trade-offs?$$,$$Decrypting TLS at a middlebox to inspect content, then re-encrypting; finds hidden threats but adds privacy, performance, and certificate-trust complexity.$$),
('f3-027','core',3,'3.3',$$Data states: at rest, in transit, in use - one protection for each?$$,$$At rest: disk/database encryption. In transit: TLS/IPSec. In use: secure enclaves/access controls (hardest to protect).$$),
('f3-028','core',3,'3.3',$$Regulated data vs trade secrets vs intellectual property?$$,$$Regulated: protection required by law (PII, PHI, card data). Trade secret: confidential business know-how. IP: creations protected by patent/copyright/trademark.$$),
('f3-029','core',3,'3.3',$$Data classification levels (typical commercial)?$$,$$Public, Internal/Private, Confidential, Restricted/Critical - protections scale with sensitivity.$$),
('f3-030','core',3,'3.3',$$What is data sovereignty?$$,$$Data is subject to the laws of the country where it is physically stored - drives geographic restrictions on storage/processing.$$),
('f3-031','core',3,'3.3',$$What is geofencing/geographic restriction for data?$$,$$Controlling access or storage location by geography (block logins from regions, keep data in-country for sovereignty).$$),
('f3-032','core',3,'3.3',$$Obfuscation methods for data: masking vs tokenization vs hashing?$$,$$Masking: hide characters for display. Tokenization: substitute token, original in secure vault (reversible via vault). Hashing: one-way fingerprint (not reversible).$$),
('f3-033','core',3,'3.3',$$Ways to prevent data exfiltration by segmentation and permission?$$,$$Segment networks holding sensitive data, restrict permissions (least privilege), plus DLP monitoring egress channels (email, web, USB).$$),
('f3-034','core',3,'3.4',$$Hot vs warm vs cold site?$$,$$Hot: fully equipped + near-real-time data, minutes-hours to switch, priciest. Warm: equipment but stale data, hours-days. Cold: space/power only, days-weeks, cheapest.$$),
('f3-035','core',3,'3.4',$$Geographic dispersion - why?$$,$$Locating redundant sites far enough apart that one disaster (hurricane, earthquake, grid failure) cannot take out both.$$),
('f3-036','core',3,'3.4',$$RAID 0, 1, 5, 6, 10 - properties?$$,$$0: striping, no redundancy (speed only). 1: mirroring (1 disk can fail). 5: striping + parity (1 disk). 6: dual parity (2 disks). 10: mirrored stripes (fast + redundant, half capacity).$$),
('f3-037','core',3,'3.4',$$Full vs incremental vs differential backup?$$,$$Full: everything. Incremental: changes since LAST backup of any type (small, restore needs full + all incrementals). Differential: changes since last FULL (grows daily, restore needs full + latest differential).$$),
('f3-038','core',3,'3.4',$$Why offsite/immutable backups?$$,$$Ransomware and disasters destroy local/online backups; offsite + immutable (write-once) or offline copies survive. Rule of thumb: 3-2-1 (3 copies, 2 media, 1 offsite).$$),
('f3-039','core',3,'3.4',$$What is backup encryption and why?$$,$$Encrypt backups at rest and in transit - backups are complete copies of your sensitive data and a favorite theft target.$$),
('f3-040','core',3,'3.4',$$Snapshots vs backups?$$,$$Snapshot: point-in-time image (VM/filesystem), fast but usually stored with the system. Backup: independent copy, survives system loss.$$),
('f3-041','core',3,'3.4',$$What is journaling in resilience terms?$$,$$Recording transactions/changes in a log so state can be replayed/recovered to a precise point after failure.$$),
('f3-042','core',3,'3.4',$$Generator vs UPS - roles in power resilience?$$,$$UPS: instant battery bridge for short outages/graceful shutdown. Generator: long-duration power, takes seconds-minutes to start (UPS covers the gap).$$),
('f3-043','core',3,'3.4',$$What is capacity planning (people/technology/infrastructure)?$$,$$Ensuring enough staff, compute, and infrastructure to meet demand and absorb failures - prevents availability loss from overload.$$),
('f3-044','core',3,'3.4',$$Tabletop exercise vs simulation vs failover test vs parallel processing?$$,$$Tabletop: discussion walkthrough of a scenario. Simulation: acted-out drill without production impact. Failover test: actually switch to backup systems. Parallel processing: run recovery systems alongside production to validate.$$),
('f3-045','core',3,'3.4',$$Testing platform diversity/multi-cloud - what resilience problem does it address?$$,$$Avoiding a single vendor/platform as a common failure point (one provider outage or one platform vulnerability taking everything down).$$),
('f3-046','core',3,'3.2',$$Port security on switches - what does it do?$$,$$Limits which/how many MAC addresses may use a switch port; blocks rogue devices and MAC flooding.$$),
('f3-047','core',3,'3.2',$$What is a next-generation firewall (NGFW)?$$,$$Application-aware (L7) firewall integrating deep packet inspection, IPS, user identity, and often TLS inspection.$$),
('f3-048','core',3,'3.2',$$Where do you place a WAF vs a network firewall?$$,$$WAF: in front of web applications, inspecting HTTP(S) for app-layer attacks. Network firewall: at network boundaries filtering by address/port/protocol (NGFW adds app awareness).$$),
('f3-049','core',3,'3.2',$$What is a protocol-aware secure alternative list? (insecure -> secure)$$,$$Telnet->SSH, HTTP->HTTPS, FTP->SFTP/FTPS, SNMPv1/2->SNMPv3, DNS->DNSSEC/DoH, POP3/IMAP->with TLS (995/993), LDAP->LDAPS.$$),
('f3-050','core',3,'3.1',$$What is centralized vs decentralized architecture (exam view)?$$,$$Centralized: single control point - easier management, single point of failure. Decentralized: distributed control - resilient, harder to manage consistently.$$),
('f3-051','core',3,'3.1',$$Virtualization vs containerization - isolation comparison?$$,$$VMs: hardware-level isolation via hypervisor, own OS per VM (stronger). Containers: OS-level isolation sharing host kernel (lighter, weaker boundary).$$),
('f3-052','core',3,'3.1',$$What is an embedded system (security view)?$$,$$Special-purpose computer inside a device (medical, automotive, industrial); constraints: no patching, weak auth, long life - isolate and compensate.$$),
('f3-053','core',3,'3.3',$$Human-readable vs non-human-readable data?$$,$$Human-readable: text a person can interpret (documents, source). Non-human-readable: binary/encoded (compiled code, sensor data); classification and protection must cover both.$$),
('f3-054','core',3,'3.4',$$Multi-region/cloud replication - sync vs async trade-off?$$,$$Synchronous: zero data loss, distance-limited latency. Asynchronous: any distance, may lose the last seconds/minutes (RPO > 0).$$),
('f3-055','core',3,'3.2',$$What does "device attribute: active vs passive" mean in security appliance selection?$$,$$Active devices interact with/alter traffic (IPS, inline filter - can prevent but can break traffic); passive devices only observe (IDS, tap - safe but detect-only).$$);
